// CAP-4 D-9: the universal door's classification table — the unit.
// One row per pinned POST/DELETE operation that is not in the money
// class: 215 rows (the 10 whitelist baseline rows, the 191 effective
// refused rows of the CAP-4 census SS4, the 13 v1/accounts rows flipped
// from OTHER-UNIT to the Connect family that slices 1-3 landed, the
// Stripe Apps install rows recorded as CAP-3's, and the account_links
// row flipped to its native Connect tool). The 92 money-class
// operations are NOT rows: the transfer branch of
// classifyUniversalWrite refuses them first, unchanged, and the drift
// test proves the partition is exact (307 pinned POST/DELETE =
// 92 money + 215 rows).
//
// RE-DERIVATION PROCEDURE (run on every catalog re-pin; the drift
// test in tests/unit/cap4-d9-universal-door.test.js enforces it):
//   1. Enumerate catalog().operations for POST/DELETE from
//      src/stripe/openapi-index.js (the pinned catalog).
//   2. Partition out the money class with the transfer regex in
//      src/stripe/universal-policy.js; those operations never enter
//      this table.
//   3. Every remaining operation must carry exactly one row here,
//      classified by derivation SS B.2's family rules (row17-items.md):
//      ownership class, cost class, secret class, read-back route
//      (same-path GET for item writes, companion GET for collection
//      creates, parent-item GET for state actions, same-path GET for
//      singleton settings) and disposition. A new operation with no
//      row fails the drift test — unclassified is a build failure,
//      never a silent generic refusal.
//   4. Published-only operations (present in Stripe's published
//      OpenAPI but not in the pinned catalog) enter this table only
//      when a re-pin adopts them into the catalog.
// Reasons are served verbatim by the door: a refusal that names its
// true class is classified (derivation SS B.3).

